      "title": "In Defence of  Crocs Bet",
      "fund": "Substack / Tailwind Holdings",
      "date": "October 1, 2025",
      "summary": "The author analyzes punch-card investor  17.4% fund allocation to CROX, arguing that Wall Street is severely mispricing the business's normalized cash generation. Stripping out non-operating impairments and non-cash items, Crocs generated roughly $974 million in adjusted net profits over the trailing twelve months, pricing the equity at an unjustifiably cheap ~4.7x adjusted P/E against a $4.56B market cap. Operational catalysts include aggressive capital return via a refreshed $1B buyback authorization, steady leverage reduction post-HEYDUDE acquisition, and rapid growth acceleration across Asia-Pacific.",
      "url": "https://substack.com/@tailwindholdings",
      "btn_label": "Substack Memo \u2197"
    },
    {
      "title": "A second chance at the one that got away: Crocs Inc $CROX",
      "fund": "Substack / Value Degen's Substack",
      "date": "August 8, 2025",
      "summary": "This thesis highlights Crocs' structural tailwinds from the broader societal shift toward comfort footwear and high unit-level economics driven by Jibbitz customization charms, which transform $45 clogs into $60+ basket sizes with near-100% incremental margins. Despite market fears around low-cost knockoffs and HEYDUDE acquisition headwinds, Crocs maintains strong pricing power, 30%+ YoY growth in China, and high customer retention in youth footwear. The author identifies an asymmetric opportunity to double capital as management uses immense free cash flow to buy back equity at single-digit earnings multiples.",
      "url": "https://substack.com/search/CROX%20Crocs%2C%20Inc.%20investment%20thesis",
      "btn_label": "Substack Search \u2197"
